{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md", "content": "# Protocol discipline and avoiding speculative fill rules

Continual ARC tasks require one valid action on every active turn, even after an interruption. A prose status report is not a valid substitute; when the instance remains active, choose among submit, request_demos, or finish according to the live counts and end-state rules. Do not make unrelated tool calls in the evaluator loop: they consume interaction opportunities without providing puzzle evidence.

After each demo, compare all changed and preserved cells before taking another costly action. In fill/color tasks, do not assume the output is a filled bounding rectangle or the complement of a flood-filled exterior. Those are competing hypotheses that need to explain every demo, including holes, boundaries, marker cells, and untouched regions. Consider directional propagation or selectively filled regions only when supported by cellwise evidence. An incorrect submission should prompt a meaningful rule revision, not a cosmetic redraw.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-grid-reasoning/references/session-lessons-protocol-and-fill-rule.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis", "file": "references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md", "content": "# Rejected global-fill hypothesis: preserve geometry, audit exact changes

In this Continual-ARC instance, several demos used noisy grids with one distinctive-color object. An attempted submission globally flood-filled every zero connected to the marker color, and binary feedback rejected it. The failure illustrates why connectivity alone is not sufficient evidence for recoloring: the demos preserve many background cells, and different examples show selective additions localized to patterned regions rather than blanket access from a seed.

After rejection, do not make a small arbitrary edit or repeat the same flood-fill with a different boundary. Reconstruct exact changed coordinates for every input/output pair, identify the marker object's shape and candidate repeated/translated structures, and check every unchanged background region. If the demos still support incompatible rules, request another only with a stated discriminating question; otherwise use a materially different candidate and validate it against the full evidence.", "file_type": ".md", "_source_path": "/home/ubuntu/continual-arc-baselines/.workbench/runs/adhoc-hermesagentlearner-1790545211/attempts/88b7a24e5cc74e81b4e76ed1e990732f/system/state/hermes/skills/reasoning/arc-costly-demo-analysis/references/failed-global-fill-rejection.md"}

{"success": true, "name": "arc-grid-reasoning", "file": "references/object-footprint-normalization.md", "content": "# Object-footprint normalization versus detached noise

When demos show a regular monochrome rectangular object containing sparse cells of another color, distinguish those embedded defects from detached speckles of the same color elsewhere. A cleanup rule may normalize the full object footprint to its dominant color while separately removing detached noise. Determine object boundaries from repeated geometry and compare every changed and preserved cell; do not globally recolor or delete all instances of the exceptional color. After applying the rule, verify the complete output, including untouched objects and all detached markers, against every demo.
